// Builtins in the agents namespace: the two ways a body reaches a model
// (`agent`, in the background, and `ai`, in line), plus the ones the
// Assistant's own tools are built on. See dsl/_reference/_agent.memql
// for the agent primitive itself and its migration breadcrumb -- the
// `agent X { ... }` DSL primitive it planned was retired, not shipped;
// the runtime invocation surface (these builtins) stays as-is.

// ---- builtin agent --------------------------------------------------
//
// Invoke a DSL-registered agent by name to do work. ALWAYS
// asynchronous: the call creates a v1:planner:plan with
// kind="agentInvocation", queues it, and returns the plan id
// immediately. The planner's agent loop picks the Plan up async and
// drives the agent's tool loop.
//
// Callable from any DSL context that supports builtin calls --
// automations, logic functions, policies. NEVER blocks.
//
// For blocking AI work from DSL, use `builtin ai(templateId:, data:)`
// -- the synchronous named-prompt path, declared below in this
// file. `agent()` is for agent-orchestrated, tool-using work that runs
// in the background; `ai()` is for one-shot prompt calls with no tools.
//
// Args:
//   name     The agent's registry name (e.g. "assistant" --
//            typically the roleSlug). Must match an entry in the
//            AgentRegistry; the handler returns an error listing
//            registered names when no match is found.
//   prompt   Free-form prompt describing what you want the agent
//            to do. The agent's tool loop reads this as the user's
//            instruction; references to entities (attachmentId,
//            partitionId, etc.) are resolved by the agent's tools via
//            queries.
//   partitionId  Required by the planner -- the space the Plan lives in.
//            Callers without a space context can pass the system
//            placeholder (a future iteration may relax this).
//
// Returns one MemoryNode whose payload is
//   { planId: string, agent: {name, role, roleSlug} }
// The caller can subscribe to graph.node.updated.v1:planner:plan
// filtered by row.id==<planId>, or query the Plan later via
// planById, to observe progress + read Plan.output on success.

@executor("integration.agents.invoke")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Async-invoke a DSL-registered agent by name. Opens a v1:work:goal naming the invokeAgent template and returns {goalId, runId}; the run dispatcher claims it on an agent node and runs the turn. Use ai() for blocking LLM calls; agent() is for agent-orchestrated work that runs in the background.")
builtin agent {
  name     string!
  prompt   string!
  partitionId  string!
}

// ---- builtin runAgentTurn --------------------------------------------
//
// The work spine's way to invoke an agent, with no Plan in the path
// (memql#5048). `agent()` above mints a v1:planner:plan that the planner
// loop then executes; this runs the turn directly and returns the reply,
// so a compiled work template can call it as an ordinary step.
//
// SYNCHRONOUS, and that is not a downgrade from `agent()`. The asynchrony
// `agent()` promises belongs to the RUN: createGoal returns a goal id and
// the run carries on detached, so the step calling this is already the
// background. A second layer here would be a run waiting on something it
// could not journal.
//
// ONLY ANSWERS ON AN AGENT NODE, and says so rather than returning an
// empty reply -- an empty reply is indistinguishable from an agent that
// had nothing to say, and this is a deployment fact rather than an answer.

@executor("integration.agents.runAgentTurn")
@description("Run one agent turn and return its reply. The work spine's way to invoke an agent with no Plan in the path. Synchronous: the asynchrony belongs to the run that calls it. Answers only on an agent node.")
builtin runAgentTurn {
  agentId  string!
  prompt   string!
  scopeId  string
  requireFile bool
}

// ---- builtin ai -------------------------------------------------------
//
// Call a NAMED prompt with a data object, and answer with what the model
// said. The one-shot counterpart to `runAgentTurn` above: no agent, no
// tools, no run -- one prompt, rendered and sent.
//
// RESTORED, not new. MemQL had an `ai()` expression and lost both of its
// front ends BY ACCIDENT: the projection form lived only in a hand-written
// parser a replacement never re-implemented, and the form that actually ran
// was a string match in shape steps, deleted with the legacy body grammar.
// `ai(` appears nowhere in parser.V1RetiredForms, the list naming every
// deliberately retired spelling with its replacement, so nobody decided it.
// Everything under the seam kept working the whole time, and three surfaces
// went on advertising the call to authors and models.
//
// WHAT THE CALL IS, in the layer below: resolve the prompt by name, validate
// `data` against the prompt's own body (that body IS the input schema),
// render the template, route by the prompt's @level through core/airoute,
// serve from the exact-render cache when one is warm, and journal the call so
// a work run can replay it. None of that is re-implemented in the executor.
//
// NO PROVIDER ARGUMENT, and the published vocabulary was changed to match
// rather than the other way round. A level plus the rules decide (epic
// memql#5127); a model or provider named at a call site is a release every
// time the fleet changes, and it would be a routing decision written where no
// rule can see it. The pin that survives is `@defaultProvider` ON THE PROMPT,
// checked at load -- and `TestNoPaidDefault` refuses one naming a federated
// provider, which is every concrete provider record this repo ships.
//
// NO @sdk -- and that absence IS the wire decision, in the words
// dsl/platform/builtins.memql already uses. This is a body primitive; a
// generated client method for it would be a button marked "spend money on a
// provider" with no row, no run and no owner in the path.
//
// NO @requiresCapability, deliberately, though the call does spend money. The
// caller it exists for is an automation or a logic under an authored or
// system actor -- CLIENT origin, role writer -- so the only resource that
// fits the act (`execute construct`, seeded to owner and developer as "run
// inline DSL") would refuse exactly that caller while leaving a cluster owner
// free to spend: a gate that costs the feature and buys nothing. The spending
// gate is real, it is in Go, and no annotation reaches it -- ai_guard.go's
// identical-request breaker and process-wide rate ceiling sit on the
// transport every provider client is built from, and component/work/budget.go
// applies the run's ceilings when the call is inside a run.
//
// REFUSES BY NAME, never with an empty reply, for runAgentTurn's reason: an
// unreachable runtime, an unknown prompt and a data object that does not fit
// the schema are deployment or authoring facts, and an empty answer is
// indistinguishable from a model that had nothing to say.
//
// A STATEMENT, AND NOWHERE ELSE. A builtin call is a construct call, and the
// tier manifest admits a construct call in a logic body and a step argument
// only -- so a query filter, a `refine`, a sort, and a spec or trait body all
// refuse this, and the query filter names it `lower_refused` at load
// (test/conformance/2026/negative/ai/). That is what keeps a model call out of
// a per-row position, where a page of a hundred rows would be a hundred calls.
// The same reasoning is why `ai` is a BUILTIN rather than a catalog function:
// a catalog function is admitted at every in-process position, a trigger
// filter included, which would be a model call per matching event with no
// statement journalling it (component/language/tiers/ai_builtin_position_test.go).
//
// Args:
//   templateId  The prompt to call, by name -- a `prompt X { ... }`
//               declared anywhere in the loaded tree.
//   data        The object whose fields the prompt's body declares.
//               Required, and `{}` when the prompt declares none: an
//               omitted argument would be refused one layer down as a
//               missing FIELD, which reads as the prompt being wrong.
//
// Returns one MemoryNode whose payload is
//   { prompt: string, reply: any }
// `reply` is the key `runAgentTurn` already uses for the model's words, so a
// body reads one key name across both.

@executor("integration.agents.invokePrompt")
@description("Call a named prompt with a data object and return {prompt, reply}. Resolves the prompt, validates the data against the prompt's own body, renders it, and routes by the prompt's @level -- so the call names a prompt and never a model. Synchronous, and the one-shot counterpart to runAgentTurn: no agent, no tools, no run. Refuses by name when the AI runtime is unreachable, the prompt is unknown, or the data does not fit the schema.")
builtin ai {
  templateId  string!
  data        object!
}

// ---- builtin requestUserFeedback -------------------------------------
//
// Bridges the requestUserFeedback tool (dsl/agents/tools/
// requestUserFeedback.memql) to the agents Go integration's
// `requestUserFeedback` capability. The integration transitions the
// active Plan (planId is auto-stamped by the agent streaming loop's
// agentContextStamps, so the LLM never has to supply it) to
// status=awaitingFeedback / feedbackReason=feedback_required via
// requestPlanFeedback, and returns a small ack payload
// {status:"awaiting_user", planId, kind}.
//
// Mirrors the worker integration's requestComputerUseScope /
// agentworkerRequestScope pair (the scope_elevation_required
// variant); this is the generic feedback_required variant.

@executor("integration.agents.requestUserFeedback")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Transition the active Plan to awaitingFeedback (feedbackReason=feedback_required) with a feedbackRequest{question, kind, options?, timeoutAt}. The agent calls this when it needs the user to supply missing detail mid-task; the user's answer (stamped via Plan.feedbackResponse + status->running) resumes the Plan through the existing planner re-invocation path.")
builtin requestUserFeedback {
  question     string!
  kind         string!
  options      []object
  timeoutAt    string
  agentId      string!
  ownerUserId  string!
  planId       string!
  partitionId      string
}

@executor("integration.agents.ensureForGoal")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Match-extend-or-create an agent that can handle the given goal. Reads the role + domain + tool catalogs, runs agentFactoryAnalyze, issues the appropriate write, and returns {agentId, action, reasoning}. Planner-driven callers (integrations/planner/agent_loop.go) pass `planId` so the factory can stamp `lineage.originatingPlanId` on the created specialist (memql#399); the GA-driven `ensureAgent` tool omits it -- the resulting specialist still lands with `kind=\"specialist\"` (the factory only ever creates specialists, never assistants or system agents) but without a Plan back-pointer.")
builtin ensureAgentForGoal {
  goal         string!
  ownerUserId  string!
  partitionId      string
  planId       string
}

// ---- builtin produceArtifact -----------------------------------------
//
// Backs the Assistant's `produceArtifact` tool (dsl/agents/tools/
// produceArtifact.memql). Mints a v1:planner:plan (kind="produceArtifact",
// triggerSource="user.implicit") via createPlan and returns a small
// ack {status:"delegated", planId, ack}. The plan auto-runs (the planner
// agent loop auto-starts produceArtifact plans, skipping the manual Run gate),
// so the deliverable is produced + landed in the Library asynchronously while
// the Assistant just acks and ends its turn. Mirrors the requestUserFeedback
// builtin's "mutate a Plan from the agents integration, return an ack" shape.

@executor("integration.agents.produceArtifact")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Delegate a produce-an-artifact request. Opens a v1:work:goal naming the produceArtifact template, carrying the goal + desired format/filename as its input, and returns {status:\"delegated\", goalId, runId, ack}. The run executes on an agent node; the deliverable lands in the Library asynchronously.")
builtin produceArtifact {
  goal         string!
  format       string
  filename     string
  agentId      string
  ownerUserId  string!
  partitionId      string!
}
